WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.220
Terrain changes altitude relationships and can hide the aircraft, create wind effects, or reduce emergency landing options.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:15.552
Plan clearance relative to the actual surface and obstacles, not only an abstract launch elevation.

00:00:15.552 --> 00:00:22.884
Common hazards include trees, branches, wires, towers, antennas, buildings, cranes, vehicles, people, and temporary equipment.

00:00:22.884 --> 00:00:30.660
Thin wires and small branches can be difficult for both pilots and obstacle sensors to detect.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:35.770
Launch and recovery surfaces should be stable, clear, and controlled.

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:43.546
Dust, loose debris, snow, water, slope, or vegetation can affect motors, sensors, payloads, and crew footing.

00:00:43.546 --> 00:00:46.878
Select alternate landing areas before takeoff.

00:00:46.878 --> 00:00:51.544
Structures can block sky view and distort satellite-navigation signals.

00:00:51.544 --> 00:00:58.875
Urban canyons, overhangs, cliffs, and dense vegetation may reduce position accuracy or create reflected signals.

00:00:58.875 --> 00:01:04.874
A stable position-hold display should not be treated as a permanent guarantee.

00:01:04.874 --> 00:01:13.539
Obstacle-avoidance systems have blind spots and performance limits related to size, texture, lighting, speed, direction, and sensor cleanliness.

00:01:13.539 --> 00:01:20.870
They assist the operator but do not replace visual awareness, clearance, or conservative path planning.

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:26.425
Maintain a simple route with sufficient space and an escape direction.

00:01:26.425 --> 00:01:32.423
If people, vehicles, equipment, or conditions change, pause or end the mission.

00:01:32.423 --> 00:01:37.089
A closer view is not worth losing safe clearance.
